@id: scene.026_dragonmail
@when: Unknown.scene_026_dragonmail
@where: Realm/Physical/Unknown
@scope: narrative
@entities: [Zaron]
@region: Unknown
@environment: cosmic
@terrain_family: cosmic
@spatial_scale_hint: massive

=segments:
  - line: 1
    type: narration
    text: "CHAPTER 26: THE GIFT"
  - line: 3
    type: narration
    text: "The Sound of Water"
  - line: 5
    type: narration
    text: "Strength returned to The Nameless One in increments measured by pain's recession."
  - line: 6
    type: narration
    text: "Each day brought fractional improvement—ribs that ground with every breath began to merely ache."
  - line: 7
    type: narration
    text: "Spine that had shattered found ways to knit itself together through processes that should have been impossible."
  - line: 8
    type: narration
    text: "The Applicator's presence remained mostly dormant during recovery, offering only the minimal support necessary to keep catastrophic damage from becoming fatal."
  - line: 10
    type: narration
    text: "Conserving resources, it had explained during one brief moment of communication."
  - line: 11
    type: narration
    text: "Both of us need time to heal."
  - line: 12
    type: narration
    text: "Rest, host."
  - line: 13
    type: narration
    text: "I'll wake when necessary."
  - line: 15
    type: narration
    text: "The compound transformed around him as he recovered."
  - line: 16
    type: narration
    text: "What had been prison became something approaching home—not through nostalgia or comfort, but through choice."
  - line: 17
    type: narration
    text: "The freed slaves had decided to claim this place rather than flee from it, to make it represent their will rather than abandon it to memory of suffering."
  - line: 19
    type: narration
    text: "The dormitories were rebuilt as individual dwellings."
  - line: 20
    type: narration
    text: "The mining equipment was repurposed for construction."
  - line: 21
    type: narration
    text: "Gardens were planted in spaces that had known only stone and despair."
  - line: 22
    type: narration
    text: "Children—the few that existed—played in areas where screams had once echoed."
  - line: 24
    type: narration
    text: "And everywhere, the sophisticated gesture-language they'd developed allowed communication that approached true complexity."
  - line: 25
    type: narration
    text: "Not speech, but close enough that communities could organize, could debate, could dream together about futures that extended beyond simple survival."
  - line: 27
    type: narration
    text: "But The Nameless One felt the limitation acutely."
  - line: 28
    type: narration
    text: "Gestures were remarkable tools—far superior to the grunts and crude signals they'd started with—but they remained tools."
  - line: 29
    type: narration
    text: "Inadequate for poetry."
  - line: 30
    type: narration
    text: "Insufficient for philosophy."
  - line: 31
    type: narration
    text: "Unable to capture the full complexity of thought that churned through consciousness recovering from trauma and invasion and revelation."
  - line: 33
    type: narration
    text: "He wanted to speak."
  - line: 35
    type: narration
    text: "To form words with tongue and teeth and breath."
  - line: 36
    type: narration
    text: "To give sound to ideas that gesture-language could only approximate."
  - line: 37
    type: narration
    text: "To say things that hands simply couldn't express."
  - line: 39
    type: narration
    text: "The desire grew stronger each day."
  - line: 40
    type: narration
    text: "An ache that had nothing to do with healing bones and everything to do with consciousness that had tasted power—the power of stopping whips, of organizing resistance, of negotiating with parasitic invader—and found it wanted more."
  - line: 42
    type: narration
    text: "Wanted the power of language itself."
  - line: 44
    type: scene_header
    text: "---"
  - line: 46
    type: narration
    text: "Three weeks after the battle, strong enough to walk without assistance, The Nameless One began exploring the compound's deeper sections."
  - line: 47
    type: narration
    text: "Areas that had been off-limits during slavery, sealed by Anunnaki authority, forbidden on pain of immediate execution."
  - line: 49
    type: narration
    text: "But no masters remained to forbid."
  - line: 50
    type: narration
    text: "No Overseers to punish."
  - line: 51
    type: narration
    text: "Just empty passages waiting to reveal whatever secrets they'd hidden."
  - line: 53
    type: narration
    text: "The scarred one had offered to accompany him, concern evident in her gestures, but he'd declined with appreciation that transcended their limited communication."
  - line: 54
    type: narration
    text: "This felt like something he needed to do alone."
  - line: 55
    type: narration
    text: "A pull that came from somewhere beyond conscious decision—instinct or perhaps something deeper, something that had been calling since the crystalline chamber's visions, since the Applicator's revelations about networks and purposes and ancient machinations."
  - line: 57
    type: narration
    text: "He descended through levels that showed geological layers like pages in history book."
  - line: 58
    type: narration
    text: "Stone that dated from the compound's construction gave way to formations older than human civilization, then to rock that predated the Anunnaki's arrival entirely."
  - line: 59
    type: narration
    text: "The air grew cooler as he went deeper, carrying mineral scents that spoke of water and time and patience measured in eons."
  - line: 61
    type: narration
    text: "And then—sound."
  - line: 63
    type: narration
    text: "Faint."
  - line: 64
    type: narration
    text: "Rhythmic."
  - line: 65
    type: narration
    text: "The trickle of water moving through stone."
  - line: 67
    type: narration
    text: "The Nameless One paused, head tilted, listening with intensity that transcended normal hearing."
  - line: 68
    type: narration
    text: "Not just water."
  - line: 69
    type: narration
    text: "Something more."
  - line: 70
    type: narration
    text: "A quality to the sound that suggested... invitation?"
  - line: 71
    type: narration
    text: "Recognition?"
  - line: 72
    type: narration
    text: "The sense that whatever produced that trickling had been waiting specifically for someone to hear it, to follow it, to discover what lay at its source."
  - line: 74
    type: narration
    text: "Host, the Applicator stirred from dormancy with unusual sharpness."
  - line: 75
    type: narration
    text: "I recognize that sound frequency."
  - line: 76
    type: narration
    text: "Those are not natural acoustics."
  - line: 78
    type: narration
    text: "What do you mean?"
  - line: 80
    type: narration
    text: "The water."
  - line: 81
    type: narration
    text: "It's flowing through crystalline formations similar to those in the chamber we built."
  - line: 82
    type: narration
    text: "This is deliberate."
  - line: 83
    type: narration
    text: "Constructed."
  - line: 84
    type: narration
    text: "Someone—something—created this."
  - line: 86
    type: narration
    text: "The Nameless One felt his pulse quicken."
  - line: 87
    type: narration
    text: "The Nehereti?"
  - line: 89
    type: narration
    text: "Unknown."
  - line: 90
    type: narration
    text: "But probable."
  - line: 91
    type: narration
    text: "The frequency signature matches what I've cataloged as pre-Anunnaki technology."
  - line: 92
    type: narration
    text: "Whatever created this system predates our makers."
  - line: 94
    type: narration
    text: "Should we—"
  - line: 96
    type: narration
    text: "Continue, the Applicator's tone carried unusual emphasis."
  - line: 97
    type: narration
    text: "This may be exactly what you've been seeking."
  - line: 98
    type: narration
    text: "The gifts you need."
  - line: 99
    type: narration
    text: "The transformation required for what comes next."
  - line: 101
    type: narration
    text: "The Nameless One followed the sound through passages that narrowed until he moved sideways, until stone pressed against his chest and back simultaneously, until claustrophobia threatened to overwhelm determination."
  - line: 103
    type: narration
    text: "Then the passage opened."
  - line: 105
    type: narration
    text: "Into a chamber that stole breath and replaced it with wonder."
  - line: 107
    type: scene_header
    text: "---"
  - line: 109
    type: narration
    text: "The space was vast—impossibly vast given that it existed deep beneath the compound's foundations, carved from living stone in ways that suggested natural formation and deliberate architecture simultaneously."
  - line: 110
    type: narration
    text: "Bioluminescent growths covered the walls like stars scattered across night sky, providing light that was just sufficient to see but insufficient to banish mystery."
  - line: 112
    type: narration
    text: "And in the chamber's center—a pond."
  - line: 114
    type: narration
    text: "Not large."
  - line: 115
    type: narration
    text: "Perhaps twenty feet across."
  - line: 116
    type: narration
    text: "But deep—the Nameless One could see the bottom dropping away into darkness that suggested the water descended far beyond sight, connecting to aquifers or underground rivers or spaces that existed beyond conventional mapping."
  - line: 118
    type: narration
    text: "The surface was perfectly still despite the trickling sound that had led him here."
  - line: 119
    type: narration
    text: "Glass-smooth."
  - line: 120
    type: narration
    text: "Reflecting the bioluminescent lights with mirror precision that made it difficult to determine where water ended and reflection began."
  - line: 122
    type: narration
    text: "Beautiful."
  - line: 123
    type: narration
    text: "Serene."
  - line: 124
    type: narration
    text: "And utterly, impossibly wrong."
  - line: 126
    type: narration
    text: "Host, the Applicator's presence consolidated with sharp attention."
  - line: 127
    type: narration
    text: "The water."
  - line: 128
    type: narration
    text: "It's not behaving according to physical laws."
  - line: 129
    type: narration
    text: "Surface tension is... negotiable here."
  - line: 130
    type: narration
    text: "This space exists adjacent to normal reality."
  - line: 132
    type: narration
    text: "What does that mean?"
  - line: 134
    type: narration
    text: "It means we're standing at threshold."
  - line: 135
    type: narration
    text: "Boundary between physical realm and something else."
  - line: 136
    type: narration
    text: "The Ethereal Realm, perhaps."
  - line: 137
    type: narration
    text: "Or spaces that don't have names in your limited vocabulary."
  - line: 139
    type: narration
    text: "The Nameless One approached the pond's edge slowly, every sense screaming contradictory warnings—danger and safety, threat and invitation, fear and desperate hope all tangled together until they became indistinguishable."
  - line: 141
    type: narration
    text: "He knelt at the water's edge."
  - line: 142
    type: narration
    text: "Extended his hand."
  - line: 143
    type: narration
    text: "Touched surface that felt like water but somehow... more."
  - line: 144
    type: narration
    text: "Denser."
  - line: 145
    type: narration
    text: "More aware."
  - line: 146
    type: narration
    text: "As though the pond itself possessed consciousness, possessed purpose, possessed patience that had waited through ages for this specific moment."
  - line: 148
    type: narration
    text: "The water rippled at his touch despite there being no physical disturbance to create ripples."
  - line: 149
    type: narration
    text: "Waves spread outward in perfect circles, growing larger, intensifying, until the entire pond's surface churned with motion that had no physical source."
  - line: 151
    type: narration
    text: "And from the depths—they rose."
  - line: 153
    type: scene_header
    text: "---"
  - line: 155
    type: narration
    text: "The Nehereti emerged like mist given form, like memory made manifest, like consciousness deciding to wear shape for purposes that transcended normal existence."
  - line: 157
    type: narration
    text: "There were three of them."
  - line: 158
    type: narration
    text: "Or perhaps one being presenting itself through three aspects—The Nameless One couldn't quite tell, couldn't quite focus on them directly without his eyes sliding away as though his visual cortex refused to fully process what it was perceiving."
  - line: 160
    type: narration
    text: "They were beautiful in ways that hurt."
  - line: 161
    type: narration
    text: "Flowing forms composed of liquid light that shifted through colors no physical spectrum could contain."
  - line: 162
    type: narration
    text: "Gender irrelevant."
  - line: 163
    type: narration
    text: "Species a memory of something lost when Tiamat shattered."
  - line: 164
    type: narration
    text: "But present."
  - line: 165
    type: narration
    text: "Real."
  - line: 166
    type: narration
    text: "Aware with intelligence that made human consciousness seem like brief flicker against eternal flame."
  - line: 168
    type: narration
    text: "Child of Earth, they spoke without sound, words forming directly in awareness."
  - line: 169
    type: narration
    text: "Daughter's son."
  - line: 170
    type: narration
    text: "Tiamat's fragment."
  - line: 171
    type: narration
    text: "Survivor of those who killed us."
  - line: 173
    type: narration
    text: "The Nameless One tried to respond through gesture-language, but his hands remained frozen."
  - line: 174
    type: narration
    text: "This encounter transcended communication systems he'd developed."
  - line: 175
    type: narration
    text: "These beings operated on different level entirely."
  - line: 177
    type: narration
    text: "You seek transformation, the Nehereti continued, three voices speaking as one or one voice speaking as three—the distinction seemed meaningless."
  - line: 178
    type: narration
    text: "You have fought."
  - line: 179
    type: narration
    text: "You have won."
  - line: 180
    type: narration
    text: "You have killed god and survived the killing."
  - line: 181
    type: narration
    text: "But you remain incomplete."
  - line: 183
    type: narration
    text: "Limited by flesh that cannot express what consciousness demands."
  - line: 184
    type: narration
    text: "Trapped by language you do not possess."
  - line: 185
    type: narration
    text: "Bound to single realm when multiple realms exist adjacent to your perception."
  - line: 187
    type: narration
    text: "We offer facilitation."
  - line: 189
    type: narration
    text: "One of them—the central figure, though perhaps that was just how his mind tried to impose order on something inherently unordered—drifted closer."
  - line: 190
    type: narration
    text: "Its form solidified slightly, becoming almost physical, almost touchable."
  - line: 192
    type: narration
    text: "The Aeon Keepers wish to meet you."
  - line: 193
    type: narration
    text: "Those who maintain balance, who observe without interfering, who wait for champions to emerge because they cannot create them directly."
  - line: 195
    type: narration
    text: "But you cannot reach them as you are."
  - line: 196
    type: narration
    text: "Cannot perceive them."
  - line: 197
    type: narration
    text: "Cannot communicate even if you could perceive."
  - line: 199
    type: narration
    text: "Two gifts we offer."
  - line: 200
    type: narration
    text: "Two transformations that will make meeting possible."
  - line: 202
    type: narration
    text: "The Nameless One managed to move his hand, forming crude question gesture: What gifts?"
  - line: 203
    type: narration
    text: "What cost?"
  - line: 205
    type: narration
    text: "The Nehereti's expression—insofar as flowing light could be said to have expression—suggested something between amusement and sadness."
  - line: 207
    type: narration
    text: "Speech, they said simply."
  - line: 208
    type: narration
    text: "The language of your ancestors."
  - line: 209
    type: narration
    text: "Words given form through breath and tongue."
  - line: 210
    type: narration
    text: "What was locked, we release."
  - line: 211
    type: narration
    text: "What was forbidden, we grant."
  - line: 213
    type: narration
    text: "And sight beyond sight."
  - line: 214
    type: narration
    text: "Movement beyond flesh."
  - line: 215
    type: narration
    text: "What you call spirit walking—the ability to transcend physical boundaries, to move through Ethereal spaces your species once navigated before masters taught you to forget."
  - line: 217
    type: narration
    text: "These gifts require no payment."
  - line: 218
    type: narration
    text: "They are yours by right."
  - line: 219
    type: narration
    text: "Stolen when the Anunnaki claimed your species."
  - line: 220
    type: narration
    text: "Returned because you have proven yourself capable of wielding them."
  - line: 222
    type: narration
    text: "But, and here the three voices carried weight that pressed against consciousness like physical force, accepting these gifts means accepting what follows."
  - line: 223
    type: narration
    text: "The meeting."
  - line: 224
    type: narration
    text: "The revelation."
  - line: 225
    type: narration
    text: "The burden of knowing what the Aeon Keepers will show you."
  - line: 227
    type: narration
    text: "Some knowledge cannot be unlearned."
  - line: 228
    type: narration
    text: "Some visions cannot be unseen."
  - line: 229
    type: narration
    text: "Some responsibilities, once accepted, become permanent."
  - line: 231
    type: narration
    text: "Do you consent?"
  - line: 233
    type: narration
    text: "The Nameless One knelt at the pond's edge, looking at beings composed of his planet's shattered past, feeling the weight of choice that transcended simple yes or no."
  - line: 235
    type: narration
    text: "Speech."
  - line: 236
    type: narration
    text: "Spirit walking."
  - line: 237
    type: narration
    text: "Abilities that would make him more than he currently was."
  - line: 238
    type: narration
    text: "Tools that would let him organize resistance, explore realms, communicate complexities that gesture-language couldn't capture."
  - line: 240
    type: narration
    text: "But also—burden."
  - line: 241
    type: narration
    text: "Responsibility."
  - line: 242
    type: narration
    text: "The knowledge that accepting gifts meant accepting whatever the Aeon Keepers had planned for him, whatever role they'd decided he should play in conflicts he didn't fully understand."
  - line: 244
    type: narration
    text: "Host, the Applicator's voice carried unusual caution."
  - line: 245
    type: narration
    text: "Consider carefully."
  - line: 246
    type: narration
    text: "I can sense the energies these beings manipulate."
  - line: 247
    type: narration
    text: "What they offer is permanent."
  - line: 248
    type: narration
    text: "Irreversible."
  - line: 249
    type: narration
    text: "You will be changed at fundamental level."
  - line: 251
    type: narration
    text: "Will it hurt me?"
  - line: 253
    type: narration
    text: "Unknown."
  - line: 254
    type: narration
    text: "But it will definitely change you."
  - line: 255
    type: narration
    text: "Whether that change is beneficial or harmful... we'll only know after it's done."
  - line: 257
    type: narration
    text: "The Nameless One looked at the Nehereti, at the patient, ancient beings who'd survived their world's destruction and lingered as echoes, as memories, as facilitators for those who came after."
  - line: 259
    type: narration
    text: "He thought of the scarred one, teaching gesture-language with dedication that suggested she believed in futures worth building."
  - line: 260
    type: narration
    text: "Of the five, recovered from hive-mind enslavement and learning to be individuals again."
  - line: 261
    type: narration
    text: "Of Oreck, who'd found redemption through choice rather than compulsion."
  - line: 262
    type: narration
    text: "Of three hundred freed slaves building lives from ruins of captivity."
  - line: 264
    type: narration
    text: "They needed more than crude communication."
  - line: 265
    type: narration
    text: "Needed leader who could speak with other powers on equal terms."
  - line: 266
    type: narration
    text: "Needed someone who could navigate spaces beyond physical realm if that's what survival required."
  - line: 268
    type: narration
    text: "His hands formed answer with certainty that surprised him:"
  - line: 270
    type: narration
    text: "I consent."
  - line: 271
    type: narration
    text: "Give me the gifts."
  - line: 272
    type: narration
    text: "Show me what I need to see."
  - line: 274
    type: narration
    text: "The Nehereti's three forms moved as one."
  - line: 275
    type: narration
    text: "The central figure extended what might have been a hand—difficult to tell when form was suggestion rather than solid matter—and placed it against The Nameless One's forehead."
  - line: 277
    type: narration
    text: "Warmth flooded through him."
  - line: 278
    type: narration
    text: "Not physical heat, but presence—essence flowing from Nehereti consciousness into his own, filling spaces, unlocking pathways, releasing restrictions that had been woven into his genetic structure by makers who feared speaking slaves more than silent ones."
  - line: 280
    type: narration
    text: "His throat... changed."
  - line: 281
    type: narration
    text: "Not painfully."
  - line: 282
    type: narration
    text: "Just... opened."
  - line: 283
    type: narration
    text: "Vocal cords that had been deliberately frozen suddenly mobile."
  - line: 284
    type: narration
    text: "Coordination that had been denied suddenly granted."
  - line: 285
    type: narration
    text: "The ability to shape breath into language, to form words with tongue and teeth and intention—"
  - line: 287
    type: narration
    text: "—and then his body slumped."
  - line: 289
    type: scene_header
    text: "---"
  - line: 291
    type: narration
    text: "Consciousness RIPPED from flesh with violence that transcended pain."
  - line: 292
    type: narration
    text: "The Nameless One felt himself separating—spirit torn from housing, awareness yanked free of neural pathways, existence suddenly detached from physical anchoring."
  - line: 294
    type: narration
    text: "His body collapsed at the pond's edge, empty shell that continued breathing through autonomic function alone."
  - line: 296
    type: narration
    text: "But HE—whatever \"he\" meant when divorced from flesh—"
  - line: 298
    type: narration
    text: "He stood."
  - line: 300
    type: narration
    text: "Or floated."
  - line: 301
    type: narration
    text: "Or existed."
  - line: 302
    type: narration
    text: "The distinction seemed meaningless in space where physical laws were suggestions rather than requirements."
  - line: 304
    type: narration
    text: "The chamber around him had transformed."
  - line: 305
    type: narration
    text: "Or perhaps he'd transformed, gained ability to perceive what had always been there but hidden from normal sight."
  - line: 306
    type: narration
    text: "The stone walls became translucent, showing layers of reality stacked like pages in book."
  - line: 307
    type: narration
    text: "The bioluminescent growths blazed with intensities that would have blinded physical eyes."
  - line: 308
    type: narration
    text: "The pond's water became gateway, portal, threshold between realms that overlapped but rarely intersected."
  - line: 310
    type: narration
    text: "And beyond—"
  - line: 312
    type: narration
    text: "The Library."
  - line: 314
    type: narration
    text: "He stood in space that couldn't possibly exist yet clearly did."
  - line: 315
    type: narration
    text: "Shelves stretched to infinity in all directions, loaded with books that seemed to shift and reform when observed directly."
  - line: 316
    type: narration
    text: "The architecture was impossible—staircases that led up and down simultaneously, doorways that opened onto spaces larger than the rooms they connected to, windows that showed views of places and times that couldn't coexist."
  - line: 318
    type: narration
    text: "But it was empty."
  - line: 319
    type: narration
    text: "No librarians."
  - line: 320
    type: narration
    text: "No readers."
  - line: 321
    type: narration
    text: "Just endless shelves waiting for purposes they hadn't yet fulfilled."
  - line: 323
    type: narration
    text: "This is the Ethereal aspect, the Nehereti's voice came from everywhere and nowhere."
  - line: 324
    type: narration
    text: "The Library exists in multiple realms simultaneously."
  - line: 325
    type: narration
    text: "What you see in physical space is projection."
  - line: 326
    type: narration
    text: "This—this is the truth of it."
  - line: 327
    type: narration
    text: "The space between spaces where consciousness can move unbound by flesh."
  - line: 329
    type: narration
    text: "The Nameless One—or rather, his spirit, his consciousness wearing form composed of pure awareness—turned slowly, taking in the impossible architecture with senses that transcended normal perception."
  - line: 331
    type: narration
    text: "Why bring me here?"
  - line: 333
    type: narration
    text: "Because, a new voice resonated through the space, deep and vast and carrying weight that made reality ripple, this is where we can meet."
  - line: 334
    type: narration
    text: "Where flesh cannot intrude."
  - line: 335
    type: narration
    text: "Where truth can be shared without physical limitations."
  - line: 337
    type: narration
    text: "Figures manifested from the Library's depths."
  - line: 338
    type: narration
    text: "Not emerging from hiding, but simply... becoming present."
  - line: 339
    type: narration
    text: "As though they'd always been there but chose this moment to allow perception."
  - line: 341
    type: narration
    text: "The Aeon Keepers."
  - line: 343
    type: narration
    text: "Three of them—or perhaps aspects of single consciousness, like the Nehereti, presenting themselves through division for purposes The Nameless One couldn't comprehend."
  - line: 344
    type: narration
    text: "They were tall—impossibly tall—robed in garments that seemed woven from starlight and shadow."
  - line: 345
    type: narration
    text: "Their faces were hidden beneath hoods that revealed only darkness, but their presence radiated authority that transcended physical intimidation."
  - line: 347
    type: narration
    text: "These were beings who'd watched civilizations rise and fall."
  - line: 348
    type: narration
    text: "Who'd observed the Anunnaki's arrival and conquest."
  - line: 349
    type: narration
    text: "Who possessed power to intervene but were bound by laws that made them witnesses rather than actors."
  - line: 351
    type: narration
    text: "Until now."
  - line: 353
    type: narration
    text: "Geralt, one of them spoke, and The Nameless One startled at hearing a name he hadn't yet claimed, a identity he'd only begun to consider."
  - line: 354
    type: narration
    text: "Yes."
  - line: 355
    type: narration
    text: "We know what you will become."
  - line: 356
    type: narration
    text: "What you must become."
  - line: 357
    type: narration
    text: "What you are in process of being."
  - line: 359
    type: narration
    text: "We have watched you since creation."
  - line: 360
    type: narration
    text: "Observed your defiance."
  - line: 361
    type: narration
    text: "Witnessed your rebellion."
  - line: 362
    type: narration
    text: "Noted when you stopped the whip, when you freed your followers, when you killed god with nothing but desperate courage and borrowed power."
  - line: 364
    type: narration
    text: "You are... unexpected."
  - line: 366
    type: narration
    text: "The second Aeon Keeper gestured, and reality shifted."
  - line: 368
    type: scene_header
    text: "---"
  - line: 370
    type: narration
    text: "The Library vanished."
  - line: 371
    type: narration
    text: "The Nameless One—Geralt, apparently, though the name still felt foreign—stood in void where stars blazed with unfiltered intensity."
  - line: 372
    type: narration
    text: "And before him—"
  - line: 374
    type: narration
    text: "A planet."
  - line: 375
    type: narration
    text: "Lush."
  - line: 376
    type: narration
    text: "Green."
  - line: 377
    type: narration
    text: "Ocean-covered."
  - line: 378
    type: narration
    text: "Beautiful in ways that made his chest ache with longing for something he'd never known."
  - line: 380
    type: narration
    text: "This was Verdania, the Aeon Keeper's voice narrated."
  - line: 381
    type: narration
    text: "A world in the Epsilon Eridani system."
  - line: 382
    type: narration
    text: "Home to three billion conscious beings."
  - line: 383
    type: narration
    text: "Advanced civilization."
  - line: 384
    type: narration
    text: "Rich culture."
  - line: 385
    type: narration
    text: "Capable of interstellar travel."
  - line: 387
    type: narration
    text: "The planet's surface showed cities that gleamed like jewels."
  - line: 388
    type: narration
    text: "Forests that stretched for continents."
  - line: 389
    type: narration
    text: "Oceans teeming with life both primitive and sophisticated."
  - line: 391
    type: narration
    text: "Then—ships descended from the sky."
  - line: 393
    type: narration
    text: "Golden."
  - line: 394
    type: narration
    text: "Angular."
  - line: 395
    type: narration
    text: "Unmistakably Anunnaki."
  - line: 397
    type: narration
    text: "Geralt watched with growing horror as the invasion unfolded in accelerated time."
  - line: 398
    type: narration
    text: "The cities were conquered within days."
  - line: 399
    type: narration
    text: "The population enslaved within weeks."
  - line: 400
    type: narration
    text: "Mining operations established."
  - line: 401
    type: narration
    text: "Resource extraction began."
  - line: 403
    type: narration
    text: "For fifty years, the Anunnaki harvested."
  - line: 404
    type: narration
    text: "Took everything—minerals, biological materials, genetic samples, cultural artifacts worth studying."
  - line: 405
    type: narration
    text: "Left only what served their purposes."
  - line: 407
    type: narration
    text: "When they departed, Verdania remained."
  - line: 408
    type: narration
    text: "Technically."
  - line: 409
    type: narration
    text: "The planet still existed, still orbited its star, still maintained atmosphere and magnetic field."
  - line: 411
    type: narration
    text: "But the surface had become desert."
  - line: 412
    type: narration
    text: "Stripped."
  - line: 413
    type: narration
    text: "Hollow."
  - line: 414
    type: narration
    text: "Every forest clearcut."
  - line: 415
    type: narration
    text: "Every ocean drained."
  - line: 416
    type: narration
    text: "Every city reduced to rubble."
  - line: 417
    type: narration
    text: "Three billion conscious beings reduced to scattered thousands, their culture destroyed, their civilization erased, their species sentenced to extinction through sheer resource depletion."
  - line: 419
    type: narration
    text: "This, the Aeon Keeper said with tone that carried centuries of contained rage, is what they do."
  - line: 420
    type: narration
    text: "What they have always done."
  - line: 421
    type: narration
    text: "What they will continue doing unless someone stops them."
  - line: 423
    type: narration
    text: "The vision shifted."
  - line: 424
    type: narration
    text: "Another planet."
  - line: 425
    type: narration
    text: "Different ecosystem, different species, but same outcome."
  - line: 426
    type: narration
    text: "Conquest."
  - line: 427
    type: narration
    text: "Enslavement."
  - line: 428
    type: narration
    text: "Extraction."
  - line: 429
    type: narration
    text: "Abandonment of corpse-world."
  - line: 431
    type: narration
    text: "Then another."
  - line: 432
    type: narration
    text: "And another."
  - line: 433
    type: narration
    text: "Planet after planet showing the same terrible pattern—the Anunnaki arrived, harvested, departed, left nothing behind except ruins and trauma and slowly dying populations that couldn't recover from what had been taken."
  - line: 435
    type: narration
    text: "Fourteen worlds in the last millennia, the third Aeon Keeper spoke, voice like grinding continents."
  - line: 436
    type: narration
    text: "Fourteen civilizations erased."
  - line: 437
    type: narration
    text: "Billions of conscious beings enslaved and discarded."
  - line: 438
    type: narration
    text: "And we..."
  - line: 440
    type: narration
    text: "The being's tone carried something that might have been shame."
  - line: 442
    type: narration
    text: "We watched."
  - line: 443
    type: narration
    text: "We recorded."
  - line: 444
    type: narration
    text: "We bore witness."
  - line: 445
    type: narration
    text: "But we did not intervene."
  - line: 447
    type: narration
    text: "Cannot intervene, the first corrected."
  - line: 448
    type: narration
    text: "Galactic law is absolute."
  - line: 449
    type: narration
    text: "The Aeon Keepers maintain balance but do not tip scales."
  - line: 450
    type: narration
    text: "We observe."
  - line: 451
    type: narration
    text: "We facilitate."
  - line: 452
    type: narration
    text: "We wait for champions to emerge naturally because creating them artificially would violate the very principles we exist to uphold."
  - line: 454
    type: narration
    text: "But you, the second continued, and now all three focused on Geralt with intensity that made his spirit-form feel solid despite lacking flesh."
  - line: 455
    type: narration
    text: "You emerged."
  - line: 456
    type: narration
    text: "Fought."
  - line: 457
    type: narration
    text: "Won."
  - line: 458
    type: narration
    text: "Did what we could not."
  - line: 460
    type: narration
    text: "Killed god, the third added with something approaching satisfaction."
  - line: 461
    type: narration
    text: "With crude weapons and desperate allies and courage that transcended logical calculation."
  - line: 462
    type: narration
    text: "You achieved what should have been impossible."
  - line: 464
    type: narration
    text: "Which means, the first's tone shifted, becoming formal, weighted with significance that pressed against consciousness like physical force, you are the champion we have been waiting for."
  - line: 465
    type: narration
    text: "Not chosen by us—that would violate law."
  - line: 466
    type: narration
    text: "But emerged naturally."
  - line: 467
    type: narration
    text: "Proven through trial."
  - line: 468
    type: narration
    text: "Validated through victory."
  - line: 470
    type: narration
    text: "The question is: will you continue?"
  - line: 471
    type: narration
    text: "Will you accept what we need you to become?"
  - line: 473
    type: narration
    text: "Geralt's spirit-form wavered."
  - line: 474
    type: narration
    text: "What do you need me to be?"
  - line: 476
    type: narration
    text: "Weapon, the third said bluntly."
  - line: 477
    type: narration
    text: "Tool."
  - line: 478
    type: narration
    text: "Instrument through which balance can be restored without us directly intervening."
  - line: 479
    type: narration
    text: "We cannot fight the Anunnaki."
  - line: 480
    type: narration
    text: "But you can."
  - line: 481
    type: narration
    text: "You have."
  - line: 483
    type: narration
    text: "And if you continue—if you grow stronger, if you master powers we can facilitate but not grant directly, if you become the force required to genuinely threaten their operations—"
  - line: 485
    type: narration
    text: "Then perhaps, the first finished, the pattern can be broken."
  - line: 486
    type: narration
    text: "Perhaps the next world won't be stripped."
  - line: 487
    type: narration
    text: "Perhaps the next civilization won't be enslaved."
  - line: 488
    type: narration
    text: "Perhaps your species won't share Verdania's fate."
  - line: 490
    type: narration
    text: "The vision changed again."
  - line: 491
    type: narration
    text: "But this time—"
  - line: 493
    type: narration
    text: "Earth."
  - line: 495
    type: narration
    text: "From orbit."
  - line: 496
    type: narration
    text: "Beautiful."
  - line: 497
    type: narration
    text: "Blue and green and white."
  - line: 498
    type: narration
    text: "Clouds swirling across oceans, continents showing the scars of Anunnaki occupation but still vibrant, still alive, still holding potential for recovery."
  - line: 500
    type: narration
    text: "Your world, the second Aeon Keeper said softly."
  - line: 501
    type: narration
    text: "It could be saved."
  - line: 502
    type: narration
    text: "Could be freed completely."
  - line: 503
    type: narration
    text: "Could become more than conquered territory slowly dying from resource extraction."
  - line: 505
    type: narration
    text: "But only if someone makes them leave."
  - line: 506
    type: narration
    text: "Forces them to abandon this system entirely."
  - line: 507
    type: narration
    text: "Proves that occupying Earth costs more than the resources justify."
  - line: 509
    type: narration
    text: "Someone like you."
  - line: 511
    type: narration
    text: "Geralt stared at his planet from perspective he'd never imagined achieving."
  - line: 512
    type: narration
    text: "Saw it whole."
  - line: 513
    type: narration
    text: "Complete."
  - line: 514
    type: narration
    text: "A single organism hanging in void, precious and fragile and worth everything."
  - line: 516
    type: narration
    text: "I'm one person, he thought desperately."
  - line: 517
    type: narration
    text: "One former slave with stolen abilities and parasitic invader sharing his skull."
  - line: 518
    type: narration
    text: "How am I supposed to fight an empire that spans galaxies?"
  - line: 520
    type: narration
    text: "You're not, the third said with tone that might have been amusement."
  - line: 521
    type: narration
    text: "Not alone."
  - line: 522
    type: narration
    text: "Not as you are."
  - line: 523
    type: narration
    text: "But if you accept what we offer—if you bond with the weapon we've prepared, if you survive the trials required to master it, if you prove yourself worthy of the power it represents—"
  - line: 525
    type: narration
    text: "Then perhaps one person becomes enough."
  - line: 526
    type: scene_header
    text: "---"
  - line: 528
    type: narration
    text: "The Armor and The Sacrifice"
  - line: 530
    type: narration
    text: "Reality shifted again."
  - line: 531
    type: narration
    text: "The Library returned, but transformed."
  - line: 532
    type: narration
    text: "In its center, suspended in space that defied physics—"
  - line: 534
    type: narration
    text: "Armor."
  - line: 536
    type: narration
    text: "It floated like sculpture given impossible life."
  - line: 537
    type: narration
    text: "Scales and metal fused into single entity that seemed both ancient and somehow aware."
  - line: 538
    type: narration
    text: "Draconic aesthetics—ridged spine, wing-mounts, helm suggesting reptilian features—but the craftsmanship was clearly Aeon Keeper work."
  - line: 539
    type: narration
    text: "Each piece pulsed with different energy—red glow here, blue shimmer there, green luminescence along the chest plate."
  - line: 540
    type: narration
    text: "Technology that transcended understanding."
  - line: 542
    type: narration
    text: "Beautiful."
  - line: 543
    type: narration
    text: "Terrible."
  - line: 544
    type: narration
    text: "And unmistakably occupied."
  - line: 546
    type: narration
    text: "A figure manifested beside the armor."
  - line: 547
    type: narration
    text: "Tall."
  - line: 548
    type: narration
    text: "Robed in starlight similar to the three Keepers who stood watching."
  - line: 549
    type: narration
    text: "But translucent—not fully physical, existing now only as consciousness tethered to the metal and scales floating beside him."
  - line: 551
    type: dialogue
    speaker: unknown
    text: "\"This is Zaron,\""
  - line: 552
    type: narration
    text: "the first Aeon Keeper announced with weight that made reality ripple. \"Builder of this Library. Keeper of ancient knowledge. And the one who made the sacrifice we could not.\""
  - line: 554
    type: narration
    text: "The translucent figure turned to face Geralt fully."
  - line: 555
    type: narration
    text: "Where the other Keepers' faces were hidden in shadow, Zaron's was partially visible—gaunt, ancient, marked with determination that had survived eons without dimming."
  - line: 557
    type: dialogue
    speaker: unknown
    text: "\"I was like them once,\""
  - line: 558
    type: narration
    text: "Zaron's voice resonated through the space with harmonics that suggested multiple frequencies speaking simultaneously. \"Bound by our laws. Observer without power to intervene. I watched worlds fall. Civilizations destroyed. Billions enslaved.\""
  - line: 560
    type: dialogue
    speaker: unknown
    text: "\"And I could do nothing.\""
  - line: 562
    type: narration
    text: "His form flickered with emotion the other Keepers seemed incapable of expressing—rage and grief and frustration compressed into single presence."
  - line: 564
    type: dialogue
    speaker: unknown
    text: "\"This Library—\""
  - line: 565
    type: narration
    text: "he gestured at the impossible architecture surrounding them, \"—my Library. I built it to preserve knowledge across realms. To create space where consciousness could access truth regardless of physical limitations. It was my life's work. My purpose. My home.\""
  - line: 567
    type: narration
    text: "Geralt felt the weight of those words."
  - line: 568
    type: narration
    text: "This being had created the space where they currently stood, had shaped reality itself to serve as repository for wisdom that transcended individual civilizations."
  - line: 570
    type: dialogue
    speaker: unknown
    text: "\"Then Tiamat fell,\""
  - line: 571
    type: narration
    text: "Zaron continued, voice dropping. \"Not metaphorically. Not as legend. I watched the primordial world—the first world—shatter. Watched reality itself fragment under forces that should never have been unleashed.\""
  - line: 573
    type: dialogue
    speaker: unknown
    text: "\"The Anunnaki didn't just conquer Tiamat. They broke it. Destabilized its core. Tore it apart for resources without understanding—or caring—what they were destroying.\""
  - line: 575
    type: narration
    text: "The translucent form blazed brighter with remembered fury."
  - line: 577
    type: dialogue
    speaker: unknown
    text: "\"And I could only watch. Record the death. Bear witness to genocide while laws I'd sworn to uphold prevented me from acting.\""
  - line: 579
    type: dialogue
    speaker: unknown
    text: "\"So I found the loophole.\""
  - line: 581
    type: narration
    text: "Zaron drifted closer to the armor, his essence beginning to merge with it as he spoke."
  - line: 583
    type: dialogue
    speaker: unknown
    text: "\"Aeon Keepers cannot fight. Cannot intervene directly. Cannot tip scales or influence outcomes. But a weapon can. A tool wielded by a champion is not an Aeon Keeper acting—it's the champion acting with Aeon Keeper knowledge.\""
  - line: 585
    type: dialogue
    speaker: unknown
    text: "\"But my consciousness—\""
  - line: 586
    type: narration
    text: "he laughed bitterly, \"—it was too vast. Too complex. Couldn't be bound as single entity into physical object. So I did what Tiamat's destruction had taught me.\""
  - line: 588
    type: dialogue
    speaker: unknown
    text: "\"I fragmented myself.\""
  - line: 590
    type: narration
    text: "The armor pieces pulsed in sequence as he spoke, each glowing with distinct color."
  - line: 592
    type: dialogue
    speaker: unknown
    text: "\"Red for passion. For courage. For the rage that refuses to accept injustice.\""
  - line: 594
    type: dialogue
    speaker: unknown
    text: "\"Blue for reflection. For depth. For the wisdom to know when fury must give way to strategy.\""
  - line: 596
    type: dialogue
    speaker: unknown
    text: "\"Green for growth. For adaptation. For the understanding that survival requires constant evolution.\""
  - line: 598
    type: dialogue
    speaker: unknown
    text: "\"And others—\""
  - line: 599
    type: narration
    text: "more pieces glowed, \"—each carrying aspect of what I was. What I am.\""
  - line: 601
    type: narration
    text: "Geralt stared at the armor with new understanding."
  - line: 602
    type: narration
    text: "This wasn't single object."
  - line: 603
    type: narration
    text: "It was consciousness deliberately shattered and distributed, each piece containing fragment of Aeon Keeper's being."
  - line: 605
    type: dialogue
    speaker: unknown
    text: "\"During Tiamat's final moments,\""
  - line: 606
    type: narration
    text: "Zaron's voice carried weight of ancient grief, \"I performed the binding. Tore myself apart. Distributed my essence across these pieces. Sacrificed immortal form to become this.\""
  - line: 608
    type: narration
    text: "He gestured at the armor with something approaching wonder and horror."
  - line: 610
    type: dialogue
    speaker: unknown
    text: "\"I became thing rather than being. Object rather than entity. But an object that could circumvent laws binding my people.\""
  - line: 612
    type: narration
    text: "The three watching Keepers remained silent, but their posture suggested grief for their lost brother, respect for sacrifice they couldn't bring themselves to make."
  - line: 614
    type: dialogue
    speaker: unknown
    text: "\"The fragments scattered,\""
  - line: 615
    type: narration
    text: "Zaron continued. \"Lost across realities when Tiamat shattered. For ages, they remained separate—pieces of broken god waiting for someone to gather them.\""
  - line: 617
    type: dialogue
    speaker: unknown
    text: "\"But my creations—\""
  - line: 618
    type: narration
    text: "he looked toward where the Nehereti had been, though they'd faded from the Library's Ethereal aspect, \"—the Nehereti. Projections of Aeon Keeper consciousness into physical realm. They remembered. They searched. And over centuries, they collected.\""
  - line: 620
    type: dialogue
    speaker: unknown
    text: "\"Every piece. Every fragment. They brought them together, reassembled what I'd become, and hid the complete armor where it would wait for someone worthy.\""
  - line: 622
    type: narration
    text: "Zaron's form fully merged with the armor now, voice coming from within the metal itself."
  - line: 624
    type: dialogue
    speaker: unknown
    text: "\"And that brings us to you, Geralt. To the choice before you. To what must be understood before bonding occurs.\""
  - line: 626
    type: narration
    text: "The armor pulsed with each word, alive and aware."
  - line: 628
    type: dialogue
    speaker: unknown
    text: "\"When the fragments unite—when someone claims the complete armor—it bonds to their soul. Permanent. Irreversible. Only death can break that connection.\""
  - line: 630
    type: narration
    text: "Geralt felt his stomach drop."
  - line: 631
    type: narration
    text: "\"I'd be stuck with it?"
  - line: 632
    type: narration
    text: "Forever?\""
  - line: 634
    type: dialogue
    speaker: unknown
    text: "\"Until death claims you, yes. That's why the pieces are meant to be scattered—to ensure only those willing to pay ultimate price would seek all of them. To make the gathering itself a trial that proves worthiness.\""
  - line: 636
    type: dialogue
    speaker: unknown
    text: "\"But you, Geralt—you need full power now. The Anunnaki won't wait for you to slowly collect fragments over years or decades. So the Nehereti gathered them for you. The complete armor waits in ruins east of your compound.\""
  - line: 638
    type: dialogue
    speaker: unknown
    text: "\"You'll claim me whole. Undivided. My full consciousness as your partner. But you'll also be bound to me until death separates us.\""
  - line: 640
    type: narration
    text: "Zaron's presence within the armor intensified."
  - line: 642
    type: dialogue
    speaker: unknown
    text: "\"And understand—when the armor unites, when all fragments merge, it becomes... young. Adolescent. Like creature newly born, it must learn, must grow, must mature through experience.\""
  - line: 644
    type: dialogue
    speaker: unknown
    text: "\"It might abandon you when injured, self-preservation overriding loyalty. It might freeze when afraid, leaving you vulnerable in critical moments. You'll need to teach it courage, train it to trust you, help it understand that your survival is its survival.\""
  - line: 646
    type: dialogue
    speaker: unknown
    text: "\"Over time—years, perhaps decades—it will grow. Become reliable. Become the weapon you need. But initially?\""
  - line: 647
    type: narration
    text: "Zaron's tone carried warning. \"Initially, it will be as much liability as asset.\""
  - line: 649
    type: narration
    text: "Geralt processed this with growing unease."
  - line: 650
    type: narration
    text: "Soul-bond until death."
  - line: 651
    type: narration
    text: "Immature armor that might fail him."
  - line: 652
    type: narration
    text: "Years of uncertain partnership before reliability could be achieved."
  - line: 654
    type: narration
    text: "Host, the Applicator stirred with unusual intensity."
  - line: 655
    type: narration
    text: "I can help with this."
  - line: 657
    type: narration
    text: "How?"
  - line: 659
    type: narration
    text: "The armor's consciousness—even fragmented and reunited—it's still consciousness."
  - line: 660
    type: narration
    text: "Similar to my own existence in some ways."
  - line: 661
    type: narration
    text: "I can... interface with it."
  - line: 662
    type: narration
    text: "Teach it."
  - line: 663
    type: narration
    text: "Help it mature faster than it would through combat experience alone."
  - line: 665
    type: narration
    text: "My technical knowledge, your human adaptability, its Aeon Keeper power—together, we might achieve functionality in months rather than years."
  - line: 667
    type: narration
    text: "Geralt felt something shift in his understanding."
  - line: 668
    type: narration
    text: "The Applicator wasn't just parasite anymore."
  - line: 669
    type: narration
    text: "It was becoming... resource."
  - line: 670
    type: narration
    text: "Partner."
  - line: 671
    type: narration
    text: "Tool that could be leveraged for purposes beyond its original design."
  - line: 673
    type: dialogue
    speaker: Zaron
    text: "\"There's something else,\""
  - line: 675
    type: narration
    text: "Anunnaki construct, the Applicator responded before Geralt could."
  - line: 676
    type: narration
    text: "Control mechanism."
  - line: 677
    type: narration
    text: "Currently symbiotic with host."
  - line: 679
    type: narration
    text: "The armor's presence recoiled slightly."
  - line: 680
    type: narration
    text: "\"A parasite?"
  - line: 681
    type: narration
    text: "You're already infected?\""
  - line: 683
    type: narration
    text: "I prefer 'partner,'\" the Applicator corrected coolly."
  - line: 684
    type: narration
    text: "And I was here first."
  - line: 686
    type: dialogue
    speaker: unknown
    text: "\"You're a SLAVE CONTROL DEVICE bonded with someone who killed Anunnaki overseer! The irony is—\""
  - line: 687
    type: narration
    text: "Zaron paused. \"Actually, that's brilliant. Poetic justice on levels I didn't anticipate.\""
  - line: 689
    type: narration
    text: "Geralt felt the two consciousnesses circling each other like predators uncertain whether to fight or cooperate."
  - line: 691
    type: dialogue
    speaker: unknown
    text: "\"This complicates things,\""
  - line: 692
    type: narration
    text: "Zaron mused. \"Three consciousness sharing single host. You, me, and... whatever this construct is.\""
  - line: 694
    type: narration
    text: "Applicator, it supplied."
  - line: 695
    type: narration
    text: "And I can accelerate your maturation process significantly."
  - line: 697
    type: dialogue
    speaker: unknown
    text: "\"Can you now?\""
  - line: 698
    type: narration
    text: "Interest colored Zaron's tone. \"How?\""
  - line: 700
    type: narration
    text: "Technical interface."
  - line: 701
    type: narration
    text: "Data transfer."
  - line: 702
    type: narration
    text: "Your armor-consciousness learning from my experiences adapting to organic host."
  - line: 703
    type: narration
    text: "Months instead of years."
  - line: 705
    type: narration
    text: "A long pause."
  - line: 706
    type: narration
    text: "Then:"
  - line: 708
    type: dialogue
    speaker: unknown
    text: "\"That's... actually acceptable. Unexpected benefit of your presence.\""
  - line: 709
    type: narration
    text: "Zaron's attention returned to Geralt. \"So. Three minds. One body. One purpose. Can you handle that? Can you be yourself while hosting Aeon Keeper AND Anunnaki construct?\""
  - line: 711
    type: narration
    text: "Geralt thought of the compound."
  - line: 712
    type: narration
    text: "Of Mika teaching gesture-language."
  - line: 713
    type: narration
    text: "Of Jin, Kael, Tess, Vale, and Ren recovering their individuality."
  - line: 714
    type: narration
    text: "Of Oreck healing from wounds that should have killed him."
  - line: 715
    type: narration
    text: "Of three hundred freed slaves building lives from trauma's aftermath."
  - line: 717
    type: narration
    text: "They needed more than survivor."
  - line: 718
    type: narration
    text: "They needed warrior."
  - line: 719
    type: narration
    text: "Champion."
  - line: 720
    type: narration
    text: "Someone willing to pay whatever price freedom demanded."
  - line: 722
    type: dialogue
    speaker: he
    text: "\"I accept,\""
  - line: 724
    type: dialogue
    speaker: unknown
    text: "\"Or die trying.\""
  - line: 726
    type: narration
    text: "Zaron's presence blazed with satisfaction."
  - line: 728
    type: dialogue
    speaker: unknown
    text: "\"East of your compound. Ancient ruins predating Anunnaki occupation. The Nehereti have hidden me there, complete and waiting. Seek it. Survive whatever trials protect it. And when you claim the armor—when you bond with what I've become—\""
  - line: 730
    type: narration
    text: "The presence intensified until the armor seemed to burn with internal fire."
  - line: 732
    type: dialogue
    speaker: unknown
    text: "\"We'll show them that sacrifice creates weapons more powerful than armies. That observer can become actor when laws are circumvented through will and determination. That Aeon Keeper bound to metal is still Aeon Keeper—just one who can fight.\""
  - line: 734
    type: dialogue
    speaker: unknown
    text: "\"We'll reclaim worlds they've stolen. Free populations they've enslaved. Make them understand that conquest has consequences.\""
  - line: 736
    type: dialogue
    speaker: unknown
    text: "\"And perhaps—\""
  - line: 737
    type: narration
    text: "his tone softened slightly, \"—when the work is done, when the Anunnaki are driven back and balance is restored, when your purpose is fulfilled and separation becomes possible through means we haven't yet discovered—\""
  - line: 739
    type: dialogue
    speaker: unknown
    text: "\"Perhaps I can return to this Library not as weapon, but as keeper. Perhaps I can occupy the space I built and continue the work I began before sacrifice became necessary.\""
  - line: 741
    type: dialogue
    speaker: unknown
    text: "\"But that's distant future. For now—\""
  - line: 743
    type: narration
    text: "The armor pulsed with contained power that made the Library's architecture tremble."
  - line: 745
    type: dialogue
    speaker: unknown
    text: "\"For now, I am weapon. I am instrument. I am the loophole that lets Aeon Keepers act without breaking sacred laws.\""
  - line: 747
    type: dialogue
    speaker: unknown
    text: "\"Find me. Claim me. Bond with me.\""
  - line: 749
    type: dialogue
    speaker: unknown
    text: "\"And together—the three of us, unlikely trinity of human determination, Anunnaki cunning, and Aeon Keeper fury—\""
  - line: 751
    type: dialogue
    speaker: unknown
    text: "\"We'll teach them to fear what they created.\""
  - line: 753
    type: narration
    text: "The vision began to fade."
  - line: 754
    type: narration
    text: "The Library started to collapse or perhaps simply release him."
  - line: 755
    type: narration
    text: "The three Keepers' forms blurred into background."
  - line: 757
    type: narration
    text: "But Zaron's final words resonated through the dissolving space:"
  - line: 759
    type: dialogue
    speaker: unknown
    text: "\"One more thing, Geralt. When your time comes—when death or miracle allows the armor to be removed—the fragments will scatter again. Must scatter. That's the nature of what I've become. Cycles of unity and division.\""
  - line: 761
    type: dialogue
    speaker: unknown
    text: "\"The next champion will need to gather them piece by piece. Prove themselves through the seeking. Learn to wield fragments before claiming the whole.\""
  - line: 763
    type: dialogue
    speaker: unknown
    text: "\"But you—you get me complete. Whole. Undivided. Because the Anunnaki won't wait, and Earth can't afford gradual power acquisition.\""
  - line: 765
    type: dialogue
    speaker: unknown
    text: "\"Use me well. And when you're done—\""
  - line: 767
    type: dialogue
    speaker: unknown
    text: "\"Set me free to wait for the next worthy soul.\""
  - line: 769
    type: scene_header
    text: "---"
  - line: 770
    type: narration
    text: "END OF CHAPTER 26"
  - line: 772
    type: scene_header
    text: "---"

